      ":question/id": "af3535bf-626a-5961-8a1e-6a3e608fbdff",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} y + 6 x^{2} y=- 3 x y^{2}\\,.\n$$\n\nAssume $y\\ne 0$.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x)\\,,\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} y + 6 x^{2} y=- 3 x y^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} y + 6 x^{2} y + 3 x y^{2}=0 \\\\ y (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 6 x^{2} + 3 x y)=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 6 x^{2} + 3 x y=0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(3 x)}_{P(x)}y=\\underbrace{- 6 x^{2}}_{Q(x)} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 3 x y=- 6 x^{2}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=3 x$ and $Q(x)=- 6 x^{2}.$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
