    "question": {
      ":question/id": "58d10461-8feb-51d8-b688-d9835924b3d8",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Write the following first-order linear ODE in standard form.\n\n$$\n3 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 4 \\cos{(x )}=- 8 y \\sin{(x )} - 3\\,.\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A first-order linear ODE is in standard form if it is written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x).\n$$\n\nConverting the given equation to standard form, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{gathered}3 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 4 \\cos{(x )}=- 8 y \\sin{(x )} - 3 \\\\ 3 \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} + 8 y \\sin{(x )}=- 4 \\cos{(x )} - 3 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\underbrace{(\\frac{8 \\sin{(x )}}{3})}_{P(x)}y=\\underbrace{- \\frac{4 \\cos{(x )}}{3} - 1}_{Q(x)}\\end{gathered}\n$$\n\nNow, the equation is written in standard form with $P(x)=\\frac{8 \\sin{(x )}}{3}$ and $Q(x)=- \\frac{4 \\cos{(x )}}{3} - 1.$",
